{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,200p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Narrow ID bubble gating\n\n## Change\n\nUpdated `rtl/id_stage.sv` so reset still clears the entire ID/EX register. A\nflush now captures the current datapath payload while forcing `valid` low and\nthe decoded control bundle to zero. A stall without a flush holds the complete\nregister. Flush takes precedence over stall to preserve the existing load-use\nbubble behavior.\n\n## Deviations and concerns\n\nNo functional deviation from the hypothesis. The FPGA critical path still\ncrosses forwarding, EX, IF, and the register file into the ID/EX payload. The\nfull flow reported 45.608 MHz median Fmax, 3,795 LUT4s, and a CoreMark fitness\nof 101.54; this run does not demonstrate an Fmax improvement over the recorded\ncore.yaml value.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Verilator lint: pass, no warnings.\n- Formal: pass, 53 checks passed and 0 failed using the nret=1 wrapper.\n- Cosim: pass, both selftest and CoreMark passed using `NRET=1`.\n- FPGA flow: pass; all three placements completed and CoreMark validation\n  completed. Median Fmax was 45.608 MHz.\n\nThe default `make cosim` / `make fpga` build selects `NRET=2`, which cannot\ncompile this nret=1 core because it has no channel-1 RVFI ports. Setting\n`NRET=1` for those local commands matches `cores/bench/core.yaml` and passes.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
