{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Implementation notes: valid-bit fetch bubbles\n\n## Changes\n\n- `if_stage` now passes `imem_data` directly to IF/ID. Redirects and unavailable instruction fetches clear IF/ID validity without replacing the instruction bits.\n- `id_stage` clears decoded controls for an invalid IF/ID entry while preserving its instruction payload in ID/EX. The invalid entry is therefore a side-effect-free bubble.\n- `hazard_unit` qualifies load-use detection with IF/ID validity, and `core` connects that validity signal.\n\n## Deviations and concerns\n\nThe pipeline and redirect/stall priorities are unchanged. The raw instruction still drives decoder and register-file read addresses during an invalid fetch; validity only suppresses the decoded side effects and load-use hazard. This removes redirect selection from the 32-bit instruction path, though validity still controls the ID bubble controls.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Verilator lint: passed with no warnings.\n- Formal: passed, 53 checks and 0 failures, using the automatically selected nret=1 wrapper and checks.\n- Cosimulation: passed both ELF tests with `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`. The unqualified command defaults to NRET=2 and fails to compile because the contract for this core exposes only channel 0.\n- Gowin timing, all three placement options: passed. Fmax was 53.113, 53.246, and 53.246 MHz (median 53.246 MHz). The listed worst paths now run from ID/EX source-register fields through forwarding and EX ALU to the data-memory outputs.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
