{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"if [ -f cores/bench/LESSONS.md ]; then cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md; else echo '__MISSING__'; fi\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r1s1 (improvement, +711.88%): Moving DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the combinational EX ALU cone into a shared iterative divider removes rare division work from CoreMark’s critical path, and an EX wait interlock keeps the added divide latency off ordinary instructions.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r1s2 (regression, +6.27%): Fetch-side backward-branch prediction regressed CoreMark because decoding branch immediates and adding the target into the IF next-PC path can cost more timing than the loop bubbles it removes.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r2s0 (improvement, +12.82%): Decoded one-hot register-file reads with balanced masked-term OR trees can shorten Tang Nano register-to-ID/EX timing versus indexed 32:1 muxes, though the explicit network may increase register and logic-cell use.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r2s2 (regression, +5.74%): Even a tiny direct-mapped BTB puts indexed tag/counter reads and target selection on the IF next-PC path, and their timing cost outweighed saved CoreMark loop-redirect bubbles.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r3s0 (regression, -1.13%): A shared unsigned branch `<` still needs sign-bit XOR normalization and branch-polarity logic around EX resolution, so manual comparator sharing can add delay to the forwarded-operand redirect path instead of shortening it.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r3s2 (regression, -0.68%): A fixed-top RAS still adds JALR return-form decode and a stack-versus-sequential mux to the IF next-PC path, and CoreMark’s return savings did not offset that fetch-path cost.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r4s1 (regression, -14.54%): Sharing RV32M multiplication through one unsigned product saves parallel DSPs, but MULH/MULHSU then place op-dependent correction and dependent subtractions after the product, so the longer high-half path can outweigh the resource savings.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r4s2 (regression, -3.04%): Shrinking branch prediction to one registered entry removes BTB indexing, but the 32-bit PC-tag compare and taken-target mux \n…[3151 of 6223 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:dcdd76f93c8841ae]…\n counters regressed CoreMark, suggesting alias reduction was not its limiting factor and added fetch-side table width outweighed any prediction gain.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r12s0 (regression, +3.07%): A four-block carry-select ADD can map worse than inferred `a + b` because duplicated upper-byte sums still feed a multi-level carry network and per-byte selection muxes, offsetting shorter local carry chains.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r12s1 (regression, +1.30%): A two-entry fetch FIFO did not pay for itself because its queue and redirect/consume control added cost while the reported critical path still ran from MEM/WB through forwarding and EX logic to fetch-PC control, so overlapping fetch with backend stalls did not improve whole-core fitness.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r12s2 (regression, +0.35%): XOR-folding `pc[7:5]` into the eight-entry direction-table index regressed slightly, so reducing branch-index aliasing alone did not yield enough CoreMark benefit to offset the added fetch-index logic.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
