{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-001-r4s0.yaml <<'EOF'\nid: hyp-20260930-001-r4s0\ntitle: \\\"Shorten redirect hazard feedback\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The recent timing paths repeatedly run from forwarding through EX and fetch control into the hazard and decode logic. IF currently replaces the fetched instruction with a NOP on redirect or fetch stall, and the hazard unit then compares those muxed instruction fields. This carries redirect and stall control through an avoidable instruction mux before dependency detection.\nhypothesis: |\n  Feed load-use detection directly from the raw instruction-memory source-register fields and qualify the comparison with an explicit fetch-valid signal (imem_ready and not redirect). Keep the existing IF bubble behavior for the ID pipeline. This removes the IF instruction-selection mux from the forwarding-to-EX-to-redirect-to-hazard path while preserving dependency stalls for every valid fetched instruction, which should improve Fmax without adding pipeline state or changing normal IPC.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \\\"Connect raw instruction-memory register fields and their ready/redirect validity to load-use detection.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \\\"Qualify load-use dependency checks with fetch validity and compare the direct instruction fields.\\\"\nEOF\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
