{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq 'select(.id == \\\"hyp-20260930-001-r2s0\\\") | {motivation, hypothesis, critical_path, implementation_notes}' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && rg --files cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"motivation\": \"The current best implementation's reported critical path begins in the register file and reaches the ID/EX operand register through decode and control logic. The two dynamically indexed asynchronous reads can infer deep 32:1 muxes, which directly limit the clock period.\\n\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"The current best implementation's reported critical path begins in the register file and reaches the ID/EX operand register through decode and control logic. The two dynamically indexed asynchronous reads can infer deep 32:1 muxes, which directly limit the clock period.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Replace each dynamically indexed register-file read with a decoded one-hot select, per-register masked data terms, and a balanced OR reduction. This should shorten the register-array-to-ID/EX path on the Tang Nano LUT fabric. Preserve x0 behavior, synchronous writes and reset, and the same-cycle write-first bypass so instruction operand values remain unchanged.\\n\",\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"fitness_delta_pct\": 8,\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    \"changes\": [\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\",\n        \"description\": \"Implement both asynchronous read ports with one-hot register selection and balanced masked-data reduction, preserving x0 and write-first bypass semantics.\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"critical_path\": {\n    \"from\": \"cpu/u_ex/reg_q.rd_1_s0\",\n    \"to\": \"cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs1_val_31_s0\",\n    \"slack_ns\": -15.205,\n    \"arrival_ns\": 25.5,\n    \"logic_cells\": 53,\n    \"modules\": [\n      \"clock_ibuf\",\n      \"cpu/u_fwd\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex\",\n      \"cpu/u_if\",\n      \"cpu/u_rf\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation notes\\n\\n- Replaced both dynamically indexed asynchronous register-file reads with\\n  decoded selects, per-register masked terms, and a five-level balanced OR\\n  reduction. The x0 terms are tied to zero. Synchronous reset/write behavior\\n  and the same-cycle write-first bypass are unchanged.\\n- Added a guarded `core_pkg.sv` include to `core.sv` because the requested\\n  alphabetical `*.sv` lint command parses `core.sv` before the compilation-unit\\n  typedefs otherwise. Flows that already compile `core_pkg.sv` first are\\n  protected by its include guard.\\n- No test files changed.\\n\\n## Local checks\\n\\n- Verilator lint: passed with the requested command.\\n- Formal: passed, 53/53 checks, using the nret=1 wrapper and checks selected\\n  automatically from `core.yaml`. An initial invocation explicitly selected\\n  the nret=2 config and failed on the absent channel-1 port; the correctly\\n  configured rerun passed.\\n- Cosim: passed for both ELFs, including stalled CoreMark CRC validation. The\\n  initial `make cosim` build used its default `NRET=2`; rerunning as\\n  `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed.\\n- Gowin timing: place option 0 reported 49.492 MHz, 22 logic levels, 3762\\n  logic cells, 1605 registers, and 4 BSRAMs. The worst reported path still\\n  reaches an ID/EX operand register through the forwarding and register-file\\n  logic. The register count is notably higher than the existing\\n  `core.yaml` summary, so the one-hot network appears to trade area for timing.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\nThe explicit one-hot terms may prevent a compact memory implementation of the\\nregister array and increase register usage. The observed timing run shows the\\ncritical path still crosses the register-file path, so the Fmax improvement\\nshould be confirmed by the full multi-placement FPGA evaluation.\\n\"\n}\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
