{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"mkdir -p cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses && cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-002-r2s1.yaml <<'EOF'\nid: hyp-20260930-002-r2s1\ntitle: \\\"One-hot distributed register file\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current worst path starts in register-file storage and ends at the ID/EX operand register, so the asynchronous register read is now on the clock-limiting path after the divider was removed. A different physical register-file structure could shorten operand selection and improve Fmax without adding instruction latency.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the dynamically indexed register array read path with a one-hot decoded, distributed register file. Decode each 5-bit read address once, gate each stored word with its select bit, and combine the selected words through a balanced OR tree; implement writes as per-register enables while preserving x0, reset, and same-cycle write-first bypass behavior. This should replace the inferred multiport LUT-RAM read path with shallow parallel selection logic, targeting the measured register-file-to-ID/EX critical path while retaining the existing pipeline timing and forwarding semantics.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 9\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: \\\"Rebuild the register file as an explicit one-hot decoded word bank with balanced parallel read reduction, per-word write enables, x0 protection, reset, and write-first bypass.\\\"\nEOF\ncat schemas/hypothesis.schema.json && cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-002-r2s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
