{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git status --short --untracked-files=all && sed -n '1,120p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-002-r3s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260930-002-r3s1\ntitle: \"Register the fetch boundary\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current worst path runs from EX forwarding through redirect and fetch control into the ID register-file read and operand capture. The combinational fetch-to-decode connection lets EX control affect a long path across several pipeline blocks. A registered IF/ID boundary can isolate fetch and decode timing while keeping one instruction per cycle after the pipeline fills.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add an explicit IF/ID register between the instruction-memory interface and decode. Hold it with the existing pipeline interlocks, invalidate it on an unserviced fetch, and flush it on redirects. During an EX redirect, drive the instruction-memory address with the redirect target so the target instruction can be captured at that same edge. This moves the EX-to-fetch-to-register-file chain across a register boundary without adding a redirect bubble, and should improve Fmax on the measured cross-stage path.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add a registered IF/ID payload, separate its hold and invalidation conditions, and issue redirect-target fetches early enough to capture at the redirect edge.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Connect fetch readiness and pipeline hold control to the registered IF/ID boundary while preserving the existing core and RVFI interfaces.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
