{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-003-r3s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260930-003-r3s2\ntitle: \"Return address stack prediction\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The prior fetch predictors added branch decode, target arithmetic, or indexed table lookup to the fetch-to-PC path, and both reduced Fmax enough to lose overall fitness. CoreMark also uses indirect function returns, whose targets are already represented by recently executed call return addresses.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a small shift-register return-address stack that learns call and return actions when JAL/JALR instructions resolve in EX. In IF, recognize only the common JALR return form (link register as rs1, x0 as rd, zero immediate) and use the registered stack top as the predicted next PC when the stack is nonempty. Carry the prediction with the instruction and suppress EX redirect/flush only when the resolved JALR target matches it; otherwise retain normal recovery. A fixed-top register avoids indexed table and branch-target-adder delay in fetch while removing return bubbles in call-heavy CoreMark code.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Add predicted-target metadata to the fetch/decode and ID/EX pipeline bundles.\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: Predict recognized JALR returns from a fixed shift-register stack top, with sequential fetch as the fallback.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Carry the return prediction and predicted target into the EX-stage instruction record.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Train the stack from resolved link instructions and suppress redirects only for correctly predicted returns.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Connect the stack lookup and resolution update while preserving stall, flush, and retirement behavior.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
