{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'predict|branch|RAS|BTB|redirect' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -70; cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md; ls cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses | tail\"", "aggregated_output": "3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260930-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Iterative divide execution\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline's worst path crosses forwarding, EX, and the ALU, whose combinational DIV and REM operators can create a long serial arithmetic cone even when CoreMark is executing simpler operations. Moving divide and remainder out of that single-cycle path should improve clock frequency and reduce divider logic area.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline's worst path crosses forwarding, EX, and the ALU, whose combinational DIV and REM operators can create a long serial arithmetic cone even when CoreMark is executing simpler operations. Moving divide and remainder out of that single-cycle path should improve clock frequency and reduce divider logic area.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU datapaths with one iterative divider operating in the EX slot. Add a busy/valid interlock so younger instructions wait until the quotient or remainder is ready, while preserving all RV32M corner cases and existing bus-stall behavior. CoreMark's hot path is expected to use few divides, so the frequency gain should outweigh the added cycles for these operations.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 20, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove combinational divide and remainder cones and connect the shared iterative divider result to the M-operation selection.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add iterative divider state and a completion handshake that emits each divide instruction once and holds execution until its result is ready.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the execute busy interlock to hold younger fetch/decode work while allowing older pipeline entries to drain correctly.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the divide interlock with load-use, redirect, and memory backpressure control.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 20, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\":\n…[27970 of 31042 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b95d7ecf796d6ded]…\nigned branch `<` still needs sign-bit XOR normalization and branch-polarity logic around EX resolution, so manual comparator sharing can add delay to the forwarded-operand redirect path instead of shortening it.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r3s2 (regression, -0.68%): A fixed-top RAS still adds JALR return-form decode and a stack-versus-sequential mux to the IF next-PC path, and CoreMark’s return savings did not offset that fetch-path cost.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r4s1 (regression, -14.54%): Sharing RV32M multiplication through one unsigned product saves parallel DSPs, but MULH/MULHSU then place op-dependent correction and dependent subtractions after the product, so the longer high-half path can outweigh the resource savings.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r4s2 (regression, -3.04%): Shrinking branch prediction to one registered entry removes BTB indexing, but the 32-bit PC-tag compare and taken-target mux still burden the IF PC path, so this lookup simplification alone did not recover CoreMark fitness.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
