{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'import json; p=\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\"; rows=[json.loads(x) for x in open(p)];\nfor r in rows:\n if r.get(\\\"category\\\")==\\\"structural\\\":\n  cp=r.get(\\\"critical_path\\\",{}); print(r.get(\\\"id\\\"), \\\"|\\\",r.get(\\\"title\\\"),\\\"|\\\",r.get(\\\"outcome\\\"),r.get(\\\"delta_pct\\\"),\\\"|\\\",r.get(\\\"fmax_mhz\\\"),\\\"|\\\",cp.get(\\\"from\\\"),\\\"->\\\",cp.get(\\\"to\\\"),\\\"|\\\",r.get(\\\"lesson\\\",\\\"\\\"))'\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20260930-002-r1s1 | Iterative divide execution | improvement 711.88 | 44.201 | cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0 -> cpu/u_id/id_ex_w.rs2_val_0_s100 | - 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r1s1 (improvement, +711.88%): Moving DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the combinational EX ALU cone into a shared iterative divider removes rare division work from CoreMark’s critical path, and an EX wait interlock keeps the added divide latency off ordinary instructions.\nhyp-20260930-002-r2s1 | One-hot distributed register file | regression 3.95 | 45.948 | cpu/u_ex/reg_q.rd_0_s0 -> cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs1_val_15_s0 | \nhyp-20260930-002-r3s1 | Register the fetch boundary | regression 1.72 | 50.219 | cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs1_addr_0_s0 -> dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s | \nhyp-20260930-002-r4s1 | Share the M-extension multiplier | regression -14.54 | 42.612 | cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_0_s0 -> cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs2_val_23_s0 | - 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r4s1 (regression, -14.54%): Sharing RV32M multiplication through one unsigned product saves parallel DSPs, but MULH/MULHSU then place op-dependent correction and dependent subtractions after the product, so the longer high-half path can outweigh the resource savings.\nhyp-20260930-002-r5s1 | Parallel speculative address generation | regression -1.75 | 48.991 | cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_3_s0 -> cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs2_val_28_s0 | \nhyp-20260930-002-r6s1 | Resolve conditional branches in decode | regression -30.36 | 34.4 | cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs1_addr_0_s0 -> cpu/u_if/pc_9_s1 | - 2026-10-01 hyp-20260930-002-r6s1 (regression, -30.36%): Resolving branches in ID puts register-file/EX-MEM/MEM-WB bypassed operands through the comparator and target adder into the same-cycle fetch redirect, while dependency interlocks add bubbles, so this decode-to-PC cone can outweigh removing branch resolution from EX.\nhyp-20261001-002-r7s1 | Valid-bit fetch bubbles | improvement 6.78 | 53.246 | cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs2_addr_0_s0 -> dmem_3_dmem_3_0_0_s | - 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r7s1 (improvement, +6.78%): Keeping raw fetch bits and expressing redirects or stalls through IF/ID validity can shorten the EX-to-ID path by removing the 32-bit NOP mux, but invalid payloads must be decoded as side-effect-free bubbles and excluded from load-use detection.\nhyp-20261001-002-r8s1 | Two-stage EX multiplier | improvement 12.35 | 61.069 | cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs1_addr_3_s0 -> dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s | - 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r8s1 (improvement, +12.35%): Registering four 16×16 RV32M partial products before the combine and signed high-half corrections moves that DSP arithmetic off the common forwarded-operand EX/ALU cycle, and a multiply-only wait lets CoreMark pay the extra cycle only on M ops.\nhyp-20261001-002-r9s1 | Split forwarding from execute arithmetic | broken None | None | None -> None | \nhyp-20261001-002-r10s1 | Preselect and register writeback data | regression -2.14 | 65.112 | cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_1_s0 -> dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s | \nhyp-20261001-002-r11s1 | Register operands before execute | regression -8.45 | 73.295 | cpu/u_ex/u_multiplier/pp_hl_31_s1 -> dmem_3_dmem_3_0_0_s | - 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r11s1 (regression, -8.45%): Registering forwarded operands before arithmetic puts the immediately preceding instruction's result beyond the capture edge, so dependency interlocks add CoreMark cycles that can outweigh a shorter execute path.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
