{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r12s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261001-002-r12s1\ntitle: \"Decouple instruction fetch with a small FIFO\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  Fetch currently stops whenever a load, multiply, divide, or load-use interlock stalls the back end. During those cycles, ready instruction-memory transfers are discarded, and a later instruction-memory stall creates another pipeline bubble. A small fetch queue can use available bus cycles during back-end stalls and keep decode supplied through short fetch stalls.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a shallow fall-through FIFO between instruction fetch and decode. Advance the fetch PC and enqueue each ready instruction independently of decode progress, while allowing decode to consume queued instructions whenever its existing interlocks permit. Preserve the direction prediction metadata with each entry, flush queued wrong-path entries on an EX redirect, and give redirect recovery priority over enqueue. The queue should overlap fetch availability with back-end stalls and reduce bubbles from the randomized instruction-memory handshake without adding a decode cycle in the empty-queue case.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add a shallow fall-through instruction FIFO that captures ready fetches during decode stalls, drains during fetch stalls, and flushes on redirects while retaining PC and prediction metadata.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Separate fetch queue enqueue capacity from decode dequeue interlocks so back-end stalls can collect instructions without consuming the held decode entry.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Connect queue validity and consume control to decode and hazard logic while preserving redirect, stall, and RVFI ordering behavior.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
