{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"|category: structural|structural' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -40 && rg -n '\\\"id\\\"|\\\"title\\\"|\\\"outcome\\\"|\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\"|\\\"worst_path\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -100\"", "aggregated_output": "3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260930-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Iterative divide execution\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline's worst path crosses forwarding, EX, and the ALU, whose combinational DIV and REM operators can create a long serial arithmetic cone even when CoreMark is executing simpler operations. Moving divide and remainder out of that single-cycle path should improve clock frequency and reduce divider logic area.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline's worst path crosses forwarding, EX, and the ALU, whose combinational DIV and REM operators can create a long serial arithmetic cone even when CoreMark is executing simpler operations. Moving divide and remainder out of that single-cycle path should improve clock frequency and reduce divider logic area.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU datapaths with one iterative divider operating in the EX slot. Add a busy/valid interlock so younger instructions wait until the quotient or remainder is ready, while preserving all RV32M corner cases and existing bus-stall behavior. CoreMark's hot path is expected to use few divides, so the frequency gain should outweigh the added cycles for these operations.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 20, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove combinational divide and remainder cones and connect the shared iterative divider result to the M-operation selection.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add iterative divider