{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r13s1.yaml <<'EOF'\nid: hyp-20261001-002-r13s1\ntitle: \\\"Pipeline the execute arithmetic path\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's critical path starts at an execute forwarding source and crosses the ALU before reaching the memory-side output. The existing single EX stage leaves that operand selection and 32-bit arithmetic in one cycle, limiting Fmax even after branch prediction and match predecode improvements.\nhypothesis: |\n  Split EX into two pipelined execute stages, carrying instruction metadata and operands across an EX1/EX2 boundary. Divide the common 32-bit add/sub carry work into low- and high-half stages, and pipeline the associated result selection so ordinary instructions still enter and leave the execute pipeline at one per cycle. Add forwarding from the younger execute stage and update load-use, memory-stall, and redirect handling for the extra boundary; branch redirects may resolve one stage later, while the direction predictor limits their frequency. Shorter arithmetic stages should raise Fmax enough to outweigh the small added misprediction and load-use penalties.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \\\"Define the pipeline payload needed to carry split execute state and instruction metadata across the new stage boundary.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Split common add/sub carry work across EX1 and EX2 registers while preserving operation results, branch resolution, and precise trap metadata.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \\\"Wire the added execute stage and its forwarding result while retaining the existing top-level interface and RVFI retirement order.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \\\"Align dependency, redirect, multiply/divide,