{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'import json; rows=[json.loads(x) for x in open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\") if x.strip()];\nfor key in (\\\"hyp-20260930-002-r5s1\\\",\\\"hyp-20261001-001-r8s0\\\",\\\"hyp-20261001-002-r13s1\\\"):\n x=next(y for y in rows if y[\\\"id\\\"]==key); print(x[\\\"id\\\"],x[\\\"title\\\"],x.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",{}).get(\\\"hypothesis\\\"),\\\"\\\\n\\\",x.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\",\\\"\\\"))' \"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'import json; rows=[json.loads(x) for x in open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\") if x.strip()];\nfor key in (\\\"hyp-20260930-002-r5s1\\\",\\\"hyp-20261001-001-r8s0\\\",\\\"hyp-20261001-002-r13s1\\\"):\n x=next(y for y in rows if y[\\\"id\\\"]==key); print(x[\\\"id\\\"],x[\\\"title\\\"],x.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",{}).get(\\\"hypothesis\\\"),\\\"\\\\n\\\",x.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\",\\\"\\\"))' \"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20260930-002-r5s1 Parallel speculative address generation Add a dedicated EX address-generation path that computes the effective address in parallel for the ID/EX rs1 value, the EX/MEM result, and the MEM/WB writeback value. Select among the completed sums using the existing priority-correct forwarding select, then use that address for load/store outputs and the EX/MEM address field. Moving forwarding selection after the adder should remove the register comparison and operand mux from the address carry path, improving Fmax while leaving instruction timing and bus handshakes unchanged. Preserve misalignment trapping, memory masks, and all existing stall behavior.\n \n \nhyp-20261001-001-r8s0 Dedicated memory address adder Compute the effective address directly in EX as forwarded rs1 plus the decoded immediate, and capture that sum as the EX/MEM result for loads and stores. Keep the general ALU path for other operations and preserve the existing MEM-stage alignment checks, byte-lane logic, forwarding priority, and RVFI reporting. This specializes the timed dmem address path and may remove general ALU result-selection logic from its critical cone.\n \n # Implementation notes\n\n- Added a dedicated forwarded `rs1 + imm` effective-address sum in EX and\n  registered it in a new EX/MEM `mem_addr` field. MEM-stage dmem address,\n  byte-lane, alignment, and RVFI aligned-address logic now use that field.\n  The general ALU result and its existing forwarding behavior remain intact.\n- The first timing run still showed the general ALU result on the dmem output\n  path because that EX/MEM result also carries non-memory ALU results. The\n  separate address field was added to isolate the timed memory address path.\n- Concern: `dmem_addr` now reflects the dedicated effective-address sum during\n  cycles without a memory request; the read/write enables remain low then, so\n  the address is not consumed.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Verilator lint: pass.\n- Formal: pass, 53 checks passed and 0 failed.\n- Cosim: pass, 2 ELF cases tested with `NRET=1`.\n- Timing: `make timing TARGET=bench` reported 56.721 MHz and 12 logic levels;\n  its top five paths no longer ended at a dmem output. The worst reported path\n  ended at the general EX/MEM ALU-result register.\n- The plain `make cosim TARGET=bench` invocation used the build script's\n  default `NRET=2` and failed to compile against this core's `nret: 1`\n  interface. `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` compiled and passed.\n\nhyp-20261001-002-r13s1 Pipeline the execute arithmetic path Split EX into two pipelined execute stages, carrying instruction metadata and operands across an EX1/EX2 boundary. Divide the common 32-bit add/sub carry work into low- and high-half stages, and pipeline the associated result selection so ordinary instructions still enter and leave the execute pipeline at one per cycle. Add forwarding from the younger execute stage and update load-use, memory-stall, and redirect handling for the extra boundary; branch redirects may resolve one stage later, while the direction predictor limits their frequency. Shorter arithmetic stages should raise Fmax enough to outweigh the small added misprediction and load-use penalties.\n \n \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
