{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r15s1.yaml && git status --short'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261001-002-r15s1\ntitle: \"Separate memory address execution path\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current timing paths repeatedly run through forwarded EX operands, the general ALU, and the memory-address output. Loads and stores need only a base-plus-immediate sum, but currently share the ALU's operand selection and result selection with unrelated operations. A dedicated effective-address path can remove that common bus path from the general ALU structure without adding a pipeline bubble.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a dedicated EX-stage effective-address datapath for loads and stores. It uses the already-forwarded rs1 value and decoded immediate, then registers its sum in a separate EX/MEM address field; route the dmem address and memory alignment logic from that field while retaining the general ALU result for all other operations and for normal forwarding. This separates memory address timing from general ALU result selection, keeps instruction latency and forwarding priority unchanged, and should improve Fmax on the repeatedly observed EX-to-dmem path. Preserve dmem stall behavior, misalignment traps, byte masks, and RVFI's aligned memory reporting.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add a distinct effective-address field to the EX/MEM pipeline bundle.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compute forwarded rs1 plus immediate in a dedicated address path and register it separately from the general ALU result.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Drive memory addressing, alignment checks, and address-derived masks from the dedicated registered effective address.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
