{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'","aggregated_output":"- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r1s1 (improvement, +711.88%): Moving DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the combinational EX ALU cone into a shared iterative divider removes rare division work from CoreMark’s critical path, and an EX wait interlock keeps the added divide latency off ordinary instructions.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r1s2 (regression, +6.27%): Fetch-side backward-branch prediction regressed CoreMark because decoding branch immediates and adding the target into the IF next-PC path can cost more timing than the loop bubbles it removes.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r2s0 (improvement, +12.82%): Decoded one-hot register-file reads with balanced masked-term OR trees can shorten Tang Nano register-to-ID/EX timing versus indexed 32:1 muxes, though the explicit network may increase register and logic-cell use.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r2s2 (regression, +5.74%): Even a tiny direct-mapped BTB puts indexed tag/counter reads and target selection on the IF next-PC path, and their timing cost outweighed saved CoreMark loop-redirect bubbles.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r3s0 (regression, -1.13%): A shared unsigned branch `<` still needs sign-bit XOR normalization and branch-polarity logic around EX resolution, so manual comparator sharing can add delay to the forwarded-operand redirect path instead of shortening it.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r3s2 (regression, -0.68%): A fixed-top RAS still adds JALR return-form decode and a stack-versus-sequential mux to the IF next-PC path, and CoreMark’s return savings did not offset that fetch-path cost.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r4s1 (regression, -14.54%): Sharing RV32M multiplication through one unsigned product saves parallel DSPs, but MULH/MULHSU then place op-dependent correction and dependent subtractions after the product, so the longer high-half path can outweigh the resource savings.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r4s2 (regression, -3.04%): Shrinking branch prediction to one registered entry removes BTB indexing, but the 32-bit PC-tag compare and taken-target mux still burden the IF PC path, so this lookup simplification alone did not recover CoreMark fitness.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r5s0 (regression, -8.56%): On the EX-to-redirect path, one-hot forwarding replaces selector decode with per-bit masked-OR operand logic and priority suppression, whose added data gating can outweigh the removed binary mux decode.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r5s2 (regression, -26.62%): Retiming a 16-entry BTB lookup still leaves indexed tag and target reads from `next_pc` into prediction registers, while the full table adds state, so moving the result register alone did not offset its fetch cost with saved CoreMark redirects.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20260930-002-r6s1 (regression, -30.36%): Resolving branches in ID puts register-file/EX-MEM/MEM-WB bypassed operands through the comparator and target adder into the same-cycle fetch redirect, while dependency interlocks add bubbles, so this decode-to-PC cone can outweigh removing branch resolution from EX.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
