{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r7s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261001-002-r7s1\ntitle: \"Valid-bit fetch bubbles\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The critical paths repeatedly cross EX redirect, IF fetch control, and decode/register-file logic. The fetch stage currently replaces the full instruction word with a NOP on redirect or fetch stall, allowing a high-fanout control signal to affect instruction decode and operand selection before the ID/EX register.\nhypothesis: |\n  Keep the fetched instruction payload unmodified and represent redirects and unavailable fetches only with the existing IF/ID valid bit. Decode invalid entries as side-effect-free bubbles in ID, and qualify load-use detection with IF/ID validity. This preserves same-cycle branch recovery while removing the redirect-controlled 32-bit instruction mux and its downstream decode/read path, which should shorten the EX-to-ID critical path without adding branch bubbles.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Leave imem_data unmodified in the IF/ID payload and use valid alone to mark redirect and fetch-stall bubbles.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Treat invalid fetch entries as side-effect-free bubbles while retaining the fetched payload without redirect-driven decode changes.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Qualify load-use hazard detection with the IF/ID valid bit so invalid fetched payloads cannot cause stalls.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Connect IF/ID validity to the hazard unit and preserve the existing redirect, bus-stall, and retirement behavior.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
