{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id == \\\"hyp-20260930-003-r4s2\\\" or .id == \\\"hyp-20261001-003-r7s2\\\") | \\\"\\\\(.id): \\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis)\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20260930-003-r4s2: Add one branch target entry containing a PC tag, resolved target, and 2-bit saturating direction counter. IF predicts taken only when the current PC matches the saved tag and the counter predicts taken; this uses a tag equality check and target mux, without decoding the fetched instruction or indexing a table. Carry the prediction with the fetched instruction, correct the PC in EX whenever the resolved next PC differs, and train the entry on resolved conditional branches. Repeated hot loop branches should avoid their EX redirect bubbles with less lookup logic than the prior BTB, improving CoreMark iterations per cycle while limiting Fmax cost.\n\nhyp-20261001-003-r7s2: Replace the larger or full-width tagged branch target lookup with a 16-entry direct-mapped BTB indexed by fetch PC[5:2]. Each entry stores a valid bit, a six-bit partial PC tag, a target, and a two-bit saturating direction counter. IF predicts only a tag-matched conditional branch with a taken counter; EX trains entries and retains precise redirect recovery whenever the resolved next PC differs from the prediction. The smaller index mux and partial tag comparator should reduce predictor delay and state while preserving predictions for recurring CoreMark loops, improving total fitness over the prior large tagless table.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
