{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r11s2.yaml && git status --short'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261001-003-r11s2\ntitle: \"Expand the loop direction predictor\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current eight-entry direction table has no tags and indexes only with PC[4:2], so unrelated conditional branches can train the same counter. That interference can erase the benefit of learning CoreMark's loop behavior. The latest timing report places the critical path in EX, leaving room to improve predictor accuracy without adding a target lookup or immediate adder to fetch.\nhypothesis: |\n  Expand the direction-only predictor from eight to 32 two-bit saturating counters and index it with PC[6:2]. Keep the existing weakly-not-taken initialization, EX-stage training, predicted-taken immediate target formation, and precise mismatch recovery. The additional index bits should reduce aliasing between nearby static branches, improving loop prediction and iterations per cycle; the small counter array adds modest state and lookup width without reintroducing BTB tags or target muxes.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: Expand the direction counter array to 32 entries and index fetch lookups with PC[6:2].\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Widen the predictor update index to five bits so each resolved branch trains the matching entry.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Widen the predictor update-index connection between EX and IF while preserving existing prediction behavior.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
